\stxKnowl{
Suppose your student submits the work shown below. The student was trying to convert \(2 \frac{3}{4}\) to a single, improper fraction by using an area model as their guide.

 \includegraphics{assets/R1/frac-equivalence-1.png} 

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\stxKnowl{
What is incorrect about the work shown above? (Identify every incorrect step, not just the first.)

\stxOuttro{
The main issue is that their model does not have a consistent area to represent the whole (4/4). In the initial diagram for 2 3/4, the whole is both a square and a longer rectangle with four parts. In the second, it again changes to be longer, as they considered all the parts to be one larger whole split into 6 pieces. Furthermore, the pieces of the whole are not equal. In fraction models, the areas of the pieces of the whole must also all be equal.

}
}

\item
\stxKnowl{
For each mistake, describe why the student made that mistake. What were they probably thinking? What was their reasoning?

\stxOuttro{
The student may have been thinking that with fractions, we can always combine the numerator pieces, and count our numerator pieces over our total number of pieces. They may not have a robust understanding of how the parts of the whole must always be equal sizes.

}
}

\item
\stxKnowl{
What feedback would you give this student? Your feedback should not only give the correct steps to take, but also address their misconceptions.

\stxOuttro{
When working with area models, beware that how big a part of the picture is is very important. Equal values should have equal area. Since the 2 is two wholes, and each whole is 4/4, we need to draw each of those left two boxes as rectangles just like your 3/4. Doing so will show us that we have 4 + 4 + 3 = 11 shaded pieces. Since each of the pieces is a fourth, we know 2 3/4 = 11/4. It is probably helpful to just keep the space in between the wholes, so we don't accidentally interpret the picture as meaning one whole giant whole with more than 4 parts.

}
}

\end{enumerate}
}

