 \includegraphics{assets/R1/mult-dec-1.png} 

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\stxKnowl{
What is incorrect about the work shown above? (Identify every incorrect step, not just the first.)

\stxOuttro{
In the first partial product, the student multiplied 6 only by the 2, and not by 32. At the end, they brought the decimal straight down instead of moving it according to the total number of decimal places in the factors.

}
}

\item
\stxKnowl{
For each mistake, describe why the student made that mistake. What were they probably thinking? What was their reasoning?

\stxOuttro{
The student may have just forgotten the 3 in the first step, or they may have been unsure of what to do with the regrouped 1 from 2 times 6. They may have placed the decimal the way they did because that is how addition and subtraction of decimals works.

}
}

\item
\stxKnowl{
What feedback would you give this student? Your feedback should not only give the correct steps to take, but also address their misconceptions.

\stxOuttro{
When multiplying, don't forget to place your regrouped values all the way above the problem's next digit, then keep multiplying each digit of the first factor. After getting 12, you should place the 1 above the 3 in 32. Then multiply 6 by 3 to get 18, add that regrouped 1 to get 19, and place that 19 to the left of the 2. Also remember that the decimal placement for multiplication works differently from addition and subtraction. We need to put back all the decimal places we ignored. Since we ignored 2 total places from the two factors, we should move in 2 places from the right of the last digit of the product.

}
}

\end{enumerate}
}

