Determine if the number below is prime or composite. If it is prime, you must show all work from our prime testing method. If it is composite, you must defend your answer with the appropriate work and/or explanation.

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\stxKnowl{
\(139\)

\stxOuttro{
The number 139 is prime according to our test from class. According to the test, we must check if any of the primes less than or equal to \(\sqrt{139}\) can divide 139. Since \(11^2\) is less than or equal to 139 and \(13^2\) is greater than 139, \(\sqrt{139}\) must be between 11 and 13. This means we can stop testing at 11. We checked to see if any of the primes up to that point divide 139 evenly. None of them divided 139, as evidenced by the list of quotients and remainders below. Thus, 139 is prime!

\[\begin{aligned}139 \div 2 &= 69 \text{ remainder } 1 \\ 139 \div 3 &= 46 \text{ remainder } 1 \\ 139 \div 5 &= 27 \text{ remainder } 4 \\ 139 \div 7 &= 19 \text{ remainder } 6 \\ 139 \div 11 &= 12 \text{ remainder } 7 \\ \end{aligned}\]

}
}

\end{enumerate}
}

