\begin{enumerate}
\item
\stxKnowl{
If one large cube represents one unit, which type of block represents one tenth? Why? Relate your answer back to the base ten place value system.

\stxOuttro{
It takes ten tenths to create a single unit. Likewise, it takes ten flats to create a single large cube. Thus, a flat represents one tenth.

}
}

\item
\stxKnowl{
If one large cube represents one unit, which type of block represents one hundredth? Why? Relate your answer back to the base ten place value system.

\stxOuttro{
It takes one hundred hundredths to create a single unit. Likewise, it takes one hundred longs to create a single large cube. Thus, a long represents one hundredth. 

}
}

\item
\stxKnowl{
How do base-ten blocks physically demonstrate how a base-ten place value system works?

\stxOuttro{
In a base-ten place value system, each place value must be ten times the place value to its right. It takes ten units to make a long, ten longs to make a flat, and ten flats to make a large cube. Thus, the four types of base ten blocks can be used for any 4 consecutive base-ten place values. 

}
}

\end{enumerate}
}

